You should also implement a method `printSolution(int[] distances)` that takes the array of distances and prints the shortest distance from the source node to all other nodes in the graph.

Your solution should handle graphs with up to 1000 vertices and should efficiently find the shortest path using Dijkstra's algorithm.

### Solution:
```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Graph {
    private int V;
    private int[][] graph;

    public Graph(int vertices) {
        V = vertices;
        graph = new int[V][V];
    }

    public void addEdge(int source, int destination, int weight) {
        graph[source][destination] = weight;
        graph[destination][source] = weight; // For undirected graph
    }

    public int[] shortestPath(int source) {
        int[] distances = new int[V];
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[V];
        Arrays.fill(distances, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        distances[source] = 0;

        for (int count = 0; count < V - 1; count++) {
            int u = minDistance(distances, visited);
            visited[u] = true;

            for (int v = 0; v < V; v++) {
                if (!visited[v] && graph[u][v] != 0 && distances[u] != Integer.MAX_VALUE
                        && distances[u] + graph[u][v] < distances[v]) {
                    distances[v] = distances[u] + graph[u][v];
                }
            }
        }
        return distances;
    }

    private int minDistance(int[] distances, boolean[] visited) {
        int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE, minIndex = -1;
        for (int v = 0; v < V; v++) {
            if (!visited[v] && distances[v] <= min) {
                min = distances[v];
                minIndex = v;
            }
        }
        return minIndex;
    }

    public void printSolution(int[] distances) {
        System.out.println("Vertex \t Distance from Source");
        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {
            System.out.println(i + " \t " + distances[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

