	queue<string> q;
	q.push(c);
```

Your task is to complete the `bfs` function to perform the BFS traversal of the graph and populate the `comp` set with all the reachable nodes.

Complete the `bfs` function to achieve the following:
1. Use the provided `queue` to perform the BFS traversal of the graph.
2. Populate the `comp` set with all the nodes reachable from the starting node `c`.

Your implementation should handle the graph represented as an adjacency list, where `g` is a map with keys representing nodes and values representing the list of nodes reachable from the key node.

### Solution:
```cpp
void bfs(map<string, vector<string> > &g, string c, set<string> &comp) {
    queue<string> q;
    q.push(c);
    comp.insert(c);  // Add the starting node to the set

    while (!q.empty()) {
        string current = q.front();
        q.pop();

        // Traverse the adjacent nodes of the current node
        for (const string &neighbor : g[current]) {
            // If the neighbor has not been visited, add it to the set and the queue
            if (comp.find(neighbor) == comp.end()) {
                comp.insert(neighbor);
                q.push(neighbor);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In the provided solution, the BFS algorithm is implemented to traverse the graph and populate the `comp` set with all the reachable nodes from the starting node `c`. The starting node is added to the set initially, and then the algorithm iteratively explores the adjacent nodes of each visited node, adding them to the set and the queue if they have not been visited before. This ensures that all reachable nodes are included in the set after the BFS traversal.

