### Solution:
```java
public static List<String> calculateShortestRoute(Map<String, Map<String, Integer>> map, String start, String destination) {
    Map<String, Integer> distances = new HashMap<>();
    Map<String, String> previous = new HashMap<>();
    PriorityQueue<String> queue = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(distances::get));

    for (String location : map.keySet()) {
        if (location.equals(start)) {
            distances.put(location, 0);
        } else {
            distances.put(location, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        }
        queue.add(location);
    }

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
        String current = queue.poll();
        if (current.equals(destination)) {
            List<String> shortestRoute = new ArrayList<>();
            while (previous.containsKey(current)) {
                shortestRoute.add(0, current);
                current = previous.get(current);
            }
            shortestRoute.add(0, start);
            return shortestRoute;
        }

        for (String neighbor : map.get(current).keySet()) {
            int distance = distances.get(current) + map.get(current).get(neighbor);
            if (distance < distances.get(neighbor)) {
                distances.put(neighbor, distance);
                previous.put(neighbor, current);
                queue.remove(neighbor);
                queue.add(neighbor);
            }
        }
    }

    return new ArrayList<>();
}
```

This solution implements Dijkstra's algorithm to find the shortest route between the `start` and `destination` locations in the given weighted graph. It uses a priority queue to efficiently process the nodes and their distances, updating the shortest distances and previous nodes as it iterates through the graph. The function returns the shortest route as a list of locations.

