### Problem:
You are given a 2D grid representing a chessboard with dimensions `r` rows and `c` columns. Each cell of the grid is either empty or contains an obstacle. You are also given the coordinates of a starting cell `(x1, y1)` and a target cell `(x2, y2)`.

Your task is to determine if there exists a sequence of valid knight moves that can take the knight from the starting cell to the target cell, without landing on any obstacles. A valid knight move is defined as moving the knight from its current cell to a neighboring cell in one of the following ways:
- Moving two cells horizontally and one cell vertically, or
- Moving two cells vertically and one cell horizontally.

You need to implement the function `bool canReachTarget(int r, int c, vector<vector<int>>& grid, int x1, int y1, int x2, int y2)` where:
- `r` and `c` are the dimensions of the chessboard (1 <= r, c <= 1000).
- `grid` is a 2D vector representing the chessboard, where `grid[i][j]` is 1 if the cell at row `i` and column `j` contains an obstacle, and 0 if it is empty.
- `(x1, y1)` are the coordinates of the starting cell (0 <= x1 < r, 0 <= y1 < c).
- `(x2, y2)` are the coordinates of the target cell (0 <= x2 < r, 0 <= y2 < c).

The function should return true if there exists a sequence of valid knight moves from the starting cell to the target cell without landing on any obstacles, and false otherwise.

You can assume that the starting and target cells are not obstructed by obstacles.

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
using namespace std;

bool isValid(int x, int y, int r, int c, vector<vector<int>>& grid) {
    return x >= 0 && x < r && y >= 0 && y < c && grid[x][y] == 0;
}

bool canReachTarget(int r, int c, vector<vector<int>>& grid, int x1, int y1, int x2, int y2) {
    vector<int> dx = {-2, -2, -1, -1, 1, 1, 2, 2};
    vector<int> dy = {-1, 1, -2, 2, -2, 2, -1, 1};

    queue<pair<int, int>> q;
    q.push({x1, y1});
    grid[x1][y1] = 1; // Mark the starting cell as visited

    while (!q.empty()) {
        int x = q.front().first;
        int y = q.front().second;
        q.pop();

        if (x == x2 && y == y2) {
            return true; // Target cell reached
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            int nx = x + dx[i];
            int ny = y + dy[i];
            if (isValid(nx, ny, r, c, grid)) {
                q.push({nx, ny});
                grid[nx][ny] = 1; // Mark the cell as visited
            }
        }
    }

    return false; // Target cell not reachable
}
```

This solution uses a breadth-first search (BFS) algorithm to explore all possible valid knight moves from the starting cell. It marks visited cells to avoid revisiting them and returns true if the target cell is reached, and false otherwise.

