3. `top()`: Return the value of the top element without removing it.
4. `empty()`: Return true if the stack is empty, false otherwise.

You need to implement the `Stack` class in the `example.hpp` header file. The class should have the following structure:
```cpp
class Stack {
public:
    // Constructor
    Stack();

    // Destructor
    ~Stack();

    // Add an element to the top of the stack
    void push(int val);

    // Remove the element from the top of the stack and return its value
    int pop();

    // Return the value of the top element without removing it
    int top();

    // Return true if the stack is empty, false otherwise
    bool empty();
};
```

You should also write unit tests for the `Stack` class using Google Test framework. The unit tests should cover the functionality of the stack operations and handle edge cases.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `Stack` class and write comprehensive unit tests to ensure its correctness.

### Solution:
```cpp
// example.hpp
#ifndef STACK_HPP
#define STACK_HPP

#include <vector>
#include <stdexcept>

class Stack {
private:
    std::vector<int> elements;

public:
    // Constructor
    Stack() {}

    // Destructor
    ~Stack() {}

    // Add an element to the top of the stack
    void push(int val) {
        elements.push_back(val);
    }

    // Remove the element from the top of the stack and return its value
    int pop() {
        if (elements.empty()) {
            throw std::out_of_range("Stack is empty");
        }
        int val = elements.back();
        elements.pop_back();
        return val;
    }

    // Return the value of the top element without removing it
    int top() {
        if (elements.empty()) {
            throw std::out_of_range("Stack is empty");
        }
        return elements.back();
    }

    // Return true if the stack is empty, false otherwise
    bool empty() {
        return elements.empty();
    }
};

#endif
```

